const enBody = `<h1 id="top">Gemini 4 Carbon API Pricing: GA, and the Dec 1 Price Cliff</h1>

<p>Gemini 4 Carbon stopped being a preview. On <strong>November 10, 2026</strong>, Google opened it to every paying developer on the Gemini API and in Google AI Studio, at the same introductory price as Gemini 4 Argon: <strong>$2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens</strong>. It ranks number one on CursorBench 4.0, Perplexity's WANDR, and Zapier's AutomationBench at what Google calls more than 2x lower cost per task than the next best external model, and Arena.ai's Agent Arena analysis says its cost advantage over GPT-6 Astra and Claude Opus 5.5 <em>grows</em> on the hardest sessions.</p>

<p>Here is the part almost nobody wrote down: <strong>every one of those cost comparisons was measured at Google's introductory price, and that price expires on November 30.</strong> On December 1, 2026, Carbon's standard-tier rate doubles to $4/$20, its batch and flex rates double to $2/$10, and its priority rate doubles to $7.20/$36. OpenAI and Anthropic published no matching December 1 change for the three models Arena used as the comparison set. So on the day the promotion ends, the gap the benchmarks advertise roughly halves: Google's own "more than 2X lower cost per task" framing becomes roughly "2X lower", and the margin over Claude Opus 5.5 falls from 3.9x to about 1.95x.</p>

<p>This is a price-cliff article, not a launch article. APIRank covered Carbon's pricing on October 29, 2026, when it was still a Fairwind Program model developers could not call. What follows is the verified November 11 rate card, what December 1 does to the cost ranking, and how to budget around it.</p>

<h2 id="what-changed">What changed on November 10</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Carbon is live for all paying developers</strong> and in Google AI Studio, under the model ID <code>gemini-4-carbon</code>. The Fairwind Program cohort that had early access is no longer the gate.</li>
<li><strong>The introductory rate runs through November 30, 2026</strong>: $2/$10 per 1M tokens on the standard tier, $0.10 per 1M for cached input.</li>
<li><strong>Every tier doubles on December 1</strong>, including batch and flex. This is not a flagship-only change; there is no cheap tier that dodges it.</li>
<li><strong>Carbon and Argon cost exactly the same per token</strong> at every tier and on every date. Carbon's difference is tokens per turn, not dollars per token.</li>
<li><strong>Thinking levels are low, medium, and high</strong>, with medium as the default. The same per-token price applies at all three.</li>
<li><strong>A unified 1M-token context window</strong> with a default max output of 64K tokens, and long decode up to 1M tokens minus your input.</li>
<li><strong>Batch enqueued-token caps</strong> are 5 million on Tier 1, 500 million on Tier 2, and 1 billion on Tier 3.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="pricing">Pricing: every tier, both dates</h2>

<p>Carbon's published rates per 1M tokens, verified against Google's live pricing page on November 11, 2026. There is no free tier for Carbon on any date.</p>

<table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse mb-6">
<thead><tr class="bg-gray-100"><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Tier</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Input through Nov 30</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Output through Nov 30</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Input from Dec 1</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Output from Dec 1</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Cache read from Dec 1</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">Standard</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$2.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$10.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$4.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$20.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$0.20</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">Batch</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$1.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$5.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$2.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$10.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$0.10</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">Flex</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$1.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$5.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$2.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$10.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$0.10</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">Priority</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$3.60</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$18.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$7.20</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$36.00</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$0.36</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Two details in that table decide real budgets. First, cached input is a tenth of the input rate, so a workload with a large stable prompt prefix is far cheaper than its raw token count suggests. Second, <strong>cache reads double too</strong>: $0.10 becomes $0.20. A caching-heavy pipeline that looked nearly free is the workload most exposed to December 1.</p>

<p>Grounding is billed separately and identically for both Gemini 4 models: 5,000 free Google Search requests per month, shared across Gemini 3 and newer models, then $14 per 1,000 requests, capped at 1,500 per day. Google Maps grounding is the same. One customer request can trigger several searches, each charged separately. Unlike the Contributor tier preview, Carbon requests are <strong>not</strong> used to improve Google's products.</p>

<h2 id="cliff">The December 1 cliff and what it does to the cost ranking</h2>

<p>Arena.ai published this analysis on November 10, 2026, building on an Agent Arena observation from November 4. Their headline finding is that Google's models scale better on cost than their rivals as tasks get more demanding, with Carbon as the prime example. The specifics, for a typical task:</p>

<ul>
<li>GPT-6 Astra costs <strong>3.4x</strong> as much as Carbon</li>
<li>Claude Opus 5.5 costs <strong>3.9x</strong> as much</li>
<li>GPT-6.1 Sol is the cheaper option at about half of Carbon's cost (<strong>0.54x</strong>)</li>
</ul>

<p>Arena also reported what happens in the most expensive 5 percent of sessions: "Carbon's position improves against all three. Opus edges up to 4.2x, Astra's cost roughly doubles relative to Carbon, and Sol's discount shrinks." The direction matters more than the exact figures, because the expensive sessions carry most of the spend.</p>

<p>Now put those numbers next to a price list. As of November 11, 2026:</p>

<table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse mb-6">
<thead><tr class="bg-gray-100"><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Model</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Unit price today</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Per-task cost today</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Unit price from Dec 1</th><th class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-left">Per-task cost from Dec 1</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">Gemini 4 Carbon</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$2 / $10</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">baseline</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$4 / $20</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">baseline</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">GPT-6 Astra</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$10 / $50</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">3.4x</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$10 / $50 (no change)</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">about 1.7x</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">Claude Opus 5.5</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$4 / $20</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">3.9x</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$4 / $20 (no change)</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">about 1.95x</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">GPT-6.1 Sol</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$2 / $10</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">0.54x</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">$2 / $10 (no change)</td><td class="border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2">about 0.27x</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The last column assumes the number of tokens each model consumes per task does not change, which is the standard way to read a price change. Under that assumption, Carbon's per-task cost doubles on December 1 while all three rivals hold their prices, so every ratio measured against Carbon roughly halves. The "more than 2X lower cost per task" claim becomes about 2X, not more than 2X. And on the token-efficiency reading, Carbon stays ahead of Claude Opus 5.5 but with half the margin, while GPT-6.1 Sol stays the cheaper option per task.</p>

<p>There is a subtlety worth pausing on. Carbon and GPT-6.1 Sol share the <em>identical</em> unit price today, $2/$10 per 1M tokens. So Arena's 0.54x figure cannot be a price gap at all; it is a token-efficiency gap. On December 1, Carbon's unit price doubles while Sol's does not, which means the same efficiency difference now works in the opposite direction: on price alone Carbon costs twice what Sol costs before any token-efficiency adjustment is applied.</p>

<p>None of this makes Carbon a bad model. It makes the <em>timing</em> of the decision matter. Comparing Carbon against Opus 5.5 or Astra today means comparing a promotional rate against a standing rate, and the comparison changes on a fixed calendar date you can plan for rather than discover on an invoice.</p>

<h2 id="free-tier">Free tier: there is not one</h2>

<p>Carbon is not offered on the Gemini API free tier on any date, and neither is Argon. Google's documentation is unusually explicit about what that means: every token the model processes is counted and billed, <strong>including thinking tokens</strong> and internal tool system instructions and function declarations when using grounding. A request that produces a short answer can still bill a large input because the thinking budget is real input.</p>

<p>The free rate limits that exist on the Gemini API apply to the older 2.5 Pro and 2.5 Flash models, not to the Gemini 4 frontier tier. If you need a genuinely free path to evaluate frontier reasoning quality, the practical route is the AI Studio interface, which does not meter the same way, or a promotional credit through a provider that offers one. Do not plan a production budget around a free Carbon tier, because there isn't one.</p>

<h2 id="speed">Speed, thinking levels, and rate limits</h2>

<p>Carbon and Argon are billed identically, so latency is their only difference, and it comes from token consumption rather than price. Google's own model description puts Argon ahead on relative latency and calling it the efficient frontier model with faster responses and lower token spend per turn. Carbon trades that for depth: more thinking tokens per turn to explore and verify, which raises both token use and latency.</p>

<p>Inside Carbon, the thinking level is the dial. <code>low</code> uses the fewest tokens with the lowest latency, and quality can drop on hard tasks. <code>medium</code> is the default and the recommended balance for most work, including complex code and agentic use cases. <code>high</code> uses the most tokens and has the slowest first token, pairing well with long decode. Google advises running evaluations at all three and picking the lowest that clears your quality bar.</p>

<p>Here is the consequence that is easy to miss: <strong>the thinking level does not change the per-token price, and it is the only cost lever inside Carbon that does not double on December 1.</strong> If your bill is the problem, lowering the thinking level is the only move that helps immediately. The batch tier halves the rate but still doubles relative to today, and it trades latency for cost.</p>

<p>Batch is where the volume numbers live: 5 million enqueued tokens across your active jobs on Tier 1, 500 million on Tier 2, 1 billion on Tier 3, at half the standard rate at both prices. Spend-based rate limits apply on a rolling 10-minute window, $10 per 10 minutes on Tier 1 and $200 on Tiers 2 and 3, with a 429 RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED response when you exceed them. Carbon's per-minute request and token limits are account-specific and visible in AI Studio rather than on a public page, so read your own limits before designing around them.</p>

<h2 id="capabilities">Capabilities and benchmarks</h2>

<p>Carbon's published results from Google's November 10 announcement, with the measurement caveats stated plainly:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>CursorBench 4.0, WANDR, and AutomationBench</strong>: number one, at more than 2x lower cost per task than the next best external model, at introductory pricing.</li>
<li><strong>Multimodal Knowledge Agent</strong>, a new benchmark launched with Artificial Analysis for multimodal agentic workflows over dense documents: number two, and more than 6x lower in average cost per task than the top model.</li>
<li><strong>Balyasny Asset Management's General Financial Intelligence</strong> benchmark: first out of 23 frontier model configurations, spanning legal expertise, agentic tool use, coding, data engineering, and perception.</li>
<li><strong>Hebbia's E2E Financial Agentic</strong> evaluation: matched the best model tested, in pre-release evaluation.</li>
<li><strong>Cybersecurity</strong>, through the Fairwind Program: leads on SEC-bench Pro for finding, ExploitGym for exploiting, and CWE-bench v1 for patching.</li>
</ul>

<p>The capability set is broad for a text-output model. Inputs can be text, image, video, audio, and PDF. Supported: code execution, computer use, file search, function calling, grounding with Google Search and Google Maps, structured outputs, URL context, agentic video understanding, and context caching. Explicitly <em>not</em> supported: image generation, audio generation, and the Live API. Caching is implicit only.</p>

<p>Google's customer examples show the shape of work Carbon is aimed at: a multi-agent team reconstructing a 906K-triangle Three.js office model from seven planning PDFs across ten overnight loops, and a Carbon-assisted formal proof of the protocol underlying Signal, generated as millions of lines of Lean. These are long-horizon, verification-heavy tasks where a 2x per-task cost difference matters far less than finishing at all.</p>

<h2 id="limitations">Limitations</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>No free tier, and no free evaluation path through the API.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Output is text only.</strong> No image or audio generation, and no Live API.</li>
<li><strong>Explicit caching is unsupported.</strong> You get implicit caching at $0.10 per 1M today and $0.20 from December 1, and nothing more to manage.</li>
<li><strong>Thinking tokens are billed as output.</strong> The output price covers thinking tokens, so a high thinking level on a long context is the most expensive shape this model has.</li>
<li><strong>The introductory price is the biggest limitation of all.</strong> Every benchmark advantage quoted in this article was measured against a rate that expires on November 30.</li>
<li><strong>Carbon and Argon cost the same per token</strong>, so when the price cliff lands it hits both models equally. Argon keeps its advantage by using fewer tokens per turn.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="use-cases">Which model, which tier, and how to budget</h2>

<p>Pick Carbon for complex root-cause debugging, architectural design, dense multimodal synthesis over PDFs and spreadsheets, scientific reasoning, and security auditing. Pick Argon for interactive coding, day-to-day feature work, and fast production loops. Google is blunt about the split: Argon remains the ideal choice for most software engineering and knowledge work, and Carbon is built for tasks that test the limits of a model's intelligence.</p>

<p>For volume work, batch is the lever. A pipeline moving 100 million input tokens and 20 million output tokens per month costs $400 today on the standard tier, $800 from December 1 on that same tier, and $400 from December 1 on batch. Moving it to batch before the deadline holds your absolute bill at today's number instead of letting it double.</p>

<p>For interactive work, use the tier that matches your latency requirement and nothing more. Priority is 1.8 times the standard rate for latency-critical paths; flex is half the standard rate for everything that can wait. Most teams defaulting to standard are paying for priority behaviour they do not need.</p>

<pre><code is:raw>from google import genai

client = genai.Client()

interaction = client.interactions.create(
    model="gemini-4-carbon",
    input="Analyze this payment pipeline for race conditions during retries, then rewrite the transaction locks safely.",
    generation_config={
        "thinking_level": "high"
    }
)

print(interaction.output_text)
</code></pre>

<p>The same request over REST, which is how you would call it from a service that is not Python:</p>

<pre><code is:raw>curl "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/interactions" -H "x-goog-api-key: YOUR_GEMINI_API_KEY" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -X POST -d '{"model":"gemini-4-carbon","input":"Summarise this incident log and list the three highest-risk findings."}'
</code></pre>

<p>If you run several frontier models behind one key, an aggregator such as <a href="https://openrouter.ai/affiliates" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">OpenRouter</a> lists <code>google/gemini-4-carbon</code> at $2/$10 per 1M with a $0.10 cache read, matching Google's first-party rate exactly, plus a half-price <code>:batch</code> variant. A gateway such as <a href="https://portkey.ai/?ref=apirank" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Portkey</a> can put Carbon, Argon, and the non-Google models behind one routing and caching layer, which matters specifically during a price change because it lets you move traffic between tiers without a code change.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">Verdict</h2>

<p>Gemini 4 Carbon is a genuinely frontier-capable model that is now, finally, callable by anyone with a paid Gemini API key. On capability it is not a marginal upgrade: first on three independent workflow benchmarks, second on a new multimodal agentic benchmark at a fraction of the top model's cost, and first out of 23 configurations on a financial intelligence benchmark.</p>

<p>The catch is arithmetic, and it is a catch you can plan for rather than a reason to skip the model. Carbon's cost advantage in every published comparison was measured at a promotional rate that expires on November 30. From December 1, a like-for-like task against Claude Opus 5.5 goes from roughly 3.9 times cheaper to roughly 1.95 times cheaper, and against GPT-6.1 Sol Carbon's rate doubles while Sol's does not, so Sol stays the cheaper option per task. If your workload is high-volume and the model choice is close, budget the step-up now. If your workload is long-horizon and hard, where the benchmarks were won and where cost per task matters less than completion, the December 1 change is real but not decisive. And inside Carbon, the thinking level is the only dial that does not double, so lower it before you do anything else.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>

<p>Verified on November 11, 2026: Google's Gemini API pricing page (every Carbon and Argon rate and tier); Google's Gemini API model reference (the <code>gemini-4-carbon</code> capability list, token limits, consumption options); Google's "What's new in Gemini 4 Carbon" guide (thinking levels, model selection); Google's rate limits page (batch enqueued-token caps, usage tiers, spend-based limits); Google's Gemini 4 Carbon launch announcement of November 10, 2026 (benchmark claims); Arena.ai's Agent Arena cost analysis posted on X on November 10, 2026; OpenAI's API pricing page and Anthropic's platform pricing page (comparison models' rates, and the absence of a matching December 1 change); and the OpenRouter model catalog (the live <code>google/gemini-4-carbon</code> listing).</p>

<p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mt-6"><strong>Disclosure:</strong> APIRank is an independent comparison site. Pricing data in this article was verified against Google's official Gemini API documentation on 2026-11-11. We may earn affiliate commission when you sign up through links on this page, at no extra cost to you.</p>
`;
